    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d353">

                <pb facs="0441" n="1275" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg1275"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 658.119/421</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chargé in Great
                        Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Laughlin</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-06-14T16:00:00-04:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">June 14, 1918, 4 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>8244. For Sheldon [from War Trade Board]:</p>
                <p>No. 671. Embassy’s 10431, June 5, 11 a.m., your 721.<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d353-f1"/> Until the permanent Swedish agreement is ratified by the
                    Swedish Government we must, of course, continue to operate under the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">modus vivendi</hi> and will continue to allow vessels to
                    leave the United States whenever compensation ships from Sweden are named and
                    sail for this side. We are now working on matter of <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Sydic</hi> and other boats available for South America.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
